rule ROUTE105 "admin-route-without-auth" {
  lang python
  severity error

  find fn: function where fn.text ~ /@\w+\.(route|get|post|put|patch|delete)\(\s*["']\/(admin|internal)/ and not fn.text ~ /(@\w*(login|auth|permission|role|admin)\w*|Depends\(\s*\w*(auth|user|admin|login|permission)\w*)/
  report fn "an admin or internal route has no authentication decorator"

  example fire python """
    @app.route("/admin/users")  #~ error
    def list_users():
        return users()
  """
  example clean python """
    @router.get("/admin/users")
    def list_users(user=Depends(current_admin)):
        return users()
  """
  example notapplicable markdown """
    Prose has no routes.
  """
  explain """
    An admin or internal route that nothing guards is open to every client that can reach the server. The check reads Flask-style `@app.route("/admin...")` decorators and Flask and FastAPI verb decorators, and any decorator or `Depends(...)` whose name carries login, auth, permission, role or admin; frameworks with no route table (Django urlconf, Express, Spring) need an adapter and are reported Unresolved by the family, never clean.
    ## Remedy
    Add the framework's authentication decorator (for example `@login_required`) below the route decorator, and check the caller's role inside the handler.
  """
}
